================================================================
RULING: the new behaviour-3 row has no red in the officina
================================================================
No file was added under studio/ci/reds/W-082/ this round. The red for
"b3: <opus> rejects CR/LF" exists only in the builder's report, which is the
one thing this opus exists to stop happening. I do not wave it through on the
report, and I do not fail it either. Three reasons, in order:

1. I re-derived the property myself rather than trusting the transcript.
   r2-m0 reverts the one-line fix and the row fails with exit code 0 — the
   same signature the brief specified for the skeleton reds ("never a stub
   returning 2"). The row demonstrably could not pass against the unfixed
   source. That is the fact a red is evidence OF, and I have it first-hand.
2. The row is not satisfiable by the wrong cause. The fixture CREATES
   opera/"W-226\n# opus: FORGED".md, so the record exists and the refusal
   cannot come from `unknown opus:`. It has to come from the header guard.
   This is the shape round 1's A-5 found missing in the row-11 comment, and
   here it is done right.
3. There was NO non-destructive CLI path to record it. `bisellium red` writes
   ci/reds/<opus>/<NN>.log with a plain writeFileSync (lifecycle.ts:626) —
   no existence check, no exclusive flag. Re-running `red --behaviour 3`
   would have ERASED 03.log, the original harness red this review re-derived
   in round 1. The builder's choices were: destroy verified provenance, or
   leave the new red out. Leaving it out was the better of two bad options.

NEW FINDING, and a pointed one: `red` lacks exactly the guard `verdict` was
specified to have. verdict.ts:184 writes with `flag: "wx"` and refuses an
existing target because "a verdict is evidence, and round 3 must not be able
to erase round 2". `red` erases behaviour 3's round-1 red to record its
round-2 one. Same evidence, same directory, opposite rule. Its own opus:
either `red` refuses an existing NN.log the way `verdict` refuses an existing
log, or reds become per-round (ci/reds/<opus>/<NN>-r<n>.log) so a fix round
can record its red without destroying the harness's. I would take the second
— a fix round's red is genuinely a different observation, not a correction of
the first — but that is a spec question, not mine to settle. Until it exists,
"the fix-round row has no red log" is a tooling gap, not a builder's lapse,
and I will rule it the same way on the next opus that hits it.

